:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:58:\"10 best bare metal hypervisors for more powerful workflows\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:54:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/bare-metal-hypervisors/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:10:\"Bare metal\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:10:\"Hypervisor\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=352715\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:538:\"<p>Did you know that several businesses are using bare metal for their critical apps and services? In fact, the global bare metal cloud market was assessed to be worth $8.5 billion in 2023. Growth has been swift, and it’s now projected to reach $19.1 billion by 2028. That’s a compound annual growth rate of 17.4...</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/bare-metal-hypervisors/\">10 best bare metal hypervisors for more powerful workflows</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11:\"Maddy Osman\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:48368:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Did you know that several businesses are using <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/dedicated-server-hosting/bare-metal/\">bare metal</a> for their critical apps and services? In fact, the global <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/bare-metal/cloud/\">bare metal cloud</a> market was assessed to be worth <a href=\"https://www.marketsandmarkets.com/Market-Reports/bare-metal-cloud-market-153940759.html\">$8.5 billion in 2023</a>. Growth has been swift, and it’s now projected to reach $19.1 billion by 2028. That’s a compound annual growth rate of 17.4 percent within five years.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">What’s more, bare metal virtualization is transforming the way businesses approach IT infrastructure. By running virtual machines directly on physical hardware – without the need for a host operating system – organizations can maximize performance, security, and scalability.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether you&#8217;re handling resource-intensive applications or looking to boost efficiency, bare metal hypervisors offer unmatched control and flexibility.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In this article, we&#8217;ll explore what a bare metal hypervisor is, how it works, and why it&#8217;s become essential for high-performance environments.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Key points</strong>:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\">By running directly on physical hardware, bare metal hypervisors provide superior performance with minimal overhead, ideal for resource-intensive applications like AI, HPC, and real-time processing.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\">Bare metal hypervisors offer better security by isolating virtual machines from one another, reducing the risk of cross-VM attacks and improving data protection.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\">They are perfect for businesses that need to scale their IT infrastructure, as they allow for dynamic resource allocation and easy expansion to meet growing demands.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\">Whether for IoT, edge computing, or AI/ML workloads, bare metal hypervisors excel in environments that require high availability and the ability to handle complex, resource-heavy applications.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https://www.servers.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Browse bare metal on Servers.com</a></div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What is a bare metal hypervisor?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/bare-metal-hypervisor/\">bare metal hypervisor</a> is a piece of software that allows you to run multiple <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/kb/what-is-a-virtual-machine-a-tutorial/\">Virtual Machines (VMs)</a> on a physical server, while directly managing the hardware itself.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Unlike traditional <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/what-is-virtualization\">virtualization</a> setups (<a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/what-is-a-hypervisor/\">Type 2 hypervisor</a>), which rely on a host operating system to run the virtual machines, a bare metal hypervisor (Type 1 hypervisor) sits directly on the hardware (or &#8220;bare metal&#8221;) without any intermediary OS. This direct access to the physical hardware leads to better performance, lower overhead, and more efficient resource management.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Think of it like this: a hypervisor is the manager of your virtual machines, and in the case of bare metal, it&#8217;s managing everything at the most fundamental level – right on the physical machine. There’s no additional OS to bog things down, which means the hypervisor can work faster and make the most of the hardware at its disposal.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One of the key benefits of this setup is that it allows you to maximize the power and efficiency of your hardware by distributing its resources (like CPU, RAM, and storage) between several virtual environments, all while keeping them isolated from each other. This is especially important in environments that need to maintain high availability, security, or need to run demanding applications.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How does bare metal virtualization work?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal virtualization works by directly installing the hypervisor onto the physical hardware of a server, bypassing the need for a host operating system. This allows the hypervisor to have direct access to the server’s resources (such as CPU, memory, and storage), providing a more efficient and streamlined virtual environment.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Here’s a breakdown of how it works:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\"><strong>Hypervisor installation: </strong>The first step is installing the hypervisor onto the physical server. The hypervisor (Type 1) becomes the main operating system running on the machine.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\"><strong>Resource allocation: </strong>Once the hypervisor is up and running, it allocates the physical resources of the machine (like CPU cores, <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/dedicated-server/how-much-ram/\">RAM</a>, and storage) to create isolated VMs. Each VM operates independently with its own operating system, yet all VMs are still leveraging the same underlying physical hardware.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\"><strong>Virtual machine management: </strong>The hypervisor’s job is to act as the manager for these virtual machines, ensuring that each VM gets its fair share of resources while also maintaining isolation between them. This ensures that what happens on one VM doesn’t affect others, making the environment more secure and stable.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--30)\"><strong>Direct hardware access: </strong>Because the hypervisor has direct access to the physical hardware, it can allocate resources dynamically and efficiently. This means higher performance for demanding applications, especially when compared to virtual environments that rely on a host OS to manage the hardware on their behalf.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">By skipping the need for an additional OS, bare metal virtualization can squeeze more power out of your hardware.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Benefits of using a bare metal hypervisor</h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Performance boost</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Because a bare metal hypervisor interacts directly with the physical hardware, it eliminates the need for an underlying operating system that could otherwise introduce overhead. This direct access to the server’s resources ensures that virtual machines run with minimal latency and maximum efficiency.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For high-demand workloads – whether it’s AI research, real-time data processing, or hosting multiple enterprise applications – a bare metal hypervisor provides the horsepower required to get the job done without sacrificing performance.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Improved security</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Since the hypervisor is the primary interface between the hardware and virtual machines, it provides a layer of <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/securing-your-data-via-isolation/\">isolation</a> that enhances security. Each virtual machine runs independently, reducing the risk of one VM affecting another. This is especially important for organizations that need to maintain stringent security standards or handle sensitive data.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Better resource management</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal hypervisors can allocate resources like CPU, RAM, and storage dynamically based on the needs of each virtual machine. This level of resource control means that your hardware is used optimally, leading to cost savings and better scalability. It also ensures that workloads can be managed more effectively, with the flexibility to scale up or down as needed.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">High availability and reliability</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal hypervisors provide the reliability needed for applications that can’t afford to go offline. Their direct access to hardware allows for better performance tuning and management, and when combined with <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/what-is-high-availability-a-tutorial/\">high-availability</a> features like live migration or <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/high-availability-vs-fault-tolerance/\">fault tolerance</a>, bare metal hypervisors can help maintain continuous operations even during hardware failures or maintenance.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Reduced latency</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Without the intermediary layer of a host operating system, the hypervisor can pass instructions and data between virtual machines and the hardware with much less delay. This is crucial for performance-sensitive applications such as <a href=\"https://www.servers.com/industries/gaming\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">gaming</a>, media <a href=\"https://www.servers.com/industries/streaming\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">streaming</a>, and real-time data analytics.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Scalability</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether you&#8217;re scaling up to add more VMs or expanding to a <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/multi-server/\">multi-server setup</a>, bare metal hypervisors allow you to add resources or expand your infrastructure without disrupting the current environment. This makes them an excellent choice for businesses that need to stay agile and responsive in a fast-moving world.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-drawbacks-of-a-bare-metal-hypervisor\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Drawbacks of a bare metal hypervisor</h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Complexity of setup and maintenance</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal hypervisors, by design, require a higher level of expertise to set up and maintain compared to other virtualization options. Since the hypervisor operates directly on the hardware, there’s no easy fallback to a host operating system, making the installation and configuration process a bit more involved.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Additionally, ongoing maintenance, updates, and troubleshooting may require a deeper technical knowledge, which could be a hurdle for organizations without dedicated IT teams.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Limited hardware compatibility</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Not all hardware is created equal, and bare metal hypervisors can sometimes have specific hardware requirements or compatibility limitations. While modern hypervisors support a wide range of hardware, certain advanced features (such as GPU virtualization or hardware passthrough) may require specific hardware configurations.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For businesses using older or less common hardware, compatibility could be an issue, making it harder to fully leverage the benefits of bare metal virtualization.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-resource-intensive-for-smaller-environments\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Resource-intensive for smaller environments</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In small-scale or less resource-intensive environments, bare metal virtualization might be overkill. The hardware requirements and resource allocation needed to run a bare metal hypervisor efficiently could be too much for smaller operations, where a simpler, less complex virtualization solution might suffice. In these cases, businesses might find that the additional performance and flexibility aren’t necessary, and the setup could be a disproportionate investment of time and money.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Lack of flexibility with OS choices</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With a bare metal hypervisor, you&#8217;re tied to the hypervisor’s compatibility with the guest OS that you intend to run. While most bare metal hypervisors support a wide range of OS options, certain specialized operating systems or legacy systems may not be fully compatible or optimized for a bare metal setup. This could limit your flexibility in selecting the best OS for each virtual machine, especially if you have specific requirements for the applications you plan to run.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cost</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While bare metal virtualization can offer a great return on investment for high-performance environments, it’s not the cheapest option. In addition to the costs of the physical hardware and hypervisor software, you may also need to invest in additional resources for setup, maintenance, and support.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For smaller businesses or startups with limited budgets, these expenses can add up quickly. The upfront investment in a bare metal solution may be harder to justify if you don&#8217;t need the level of performance it provides.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Limited ecosystem support (compared to virtualization with host OS)</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When using a traditional virtualization approach with a host OS (like VMware or VirtualBox), there is a wide variety of management tools, add-ons, and integrations available in the ecosystem.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal hypervisors, on the other hand, may have a smaller range of third-party tools and integrations that work seamlessly with them. This could mean more manual configuration or a reliance on specific vendor tools, which might not always fit well with the rest of your infrastructure.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-best-bare-metal-hypervisors-for-high-performance\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Best bare metal hypervisors for high performance</h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-1-nutanix-ahv\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">1. Nutanix AHV</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1023\" height=\"332\" fetchpriority=\"high\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1023,h_332,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"nutanix bare metal hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475064\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1023,h_332,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794585692\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1023,h_332,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 1023w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_324,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_292,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_259,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_227,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_194,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_162,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_129,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_64,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585692/prod/hypervisor-nutanix/hypervisor-nutanix.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 1023px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.nutanix.com/products/ahv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Nutanix AHV</a> is the hypervisor built into the Nutanix Cloud Platform, managed through the Prism console. It has become one of the most popular destinations for teams <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/vmware-migration-readiness-checklist/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">leaving VMware</a>. Nutanix added more than 3,000 customers in fiscal 2026, and most of its new customers came from VMware.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">AHV was designed for hyperconverged infrastructure, but it now also runs on external storage from Dell, Everpure (formerly Pure Storage), and others. That lets teams migrate without replacing their existing storage. The trade-offs are commitment to the Nutanix stack and per-core licensing that adds up on high-core-count servers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Included at no extra cost with Nutanix Cloud Infrastructure (NCI), which is licensed per core. A free Community Edition is available for testing.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-2-vmware-vsphere-esxi\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">2. VMware vSphere/ESXi</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"368\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_368,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"vmware hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475059\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp\" data-crop=\"2.78\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_368,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794584331\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_368,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_359,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_323,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_287,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_251,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_215,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_179,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_143,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_107,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_71,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584331/prod/hypervisor-1/hypervisor-1.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.vmware.com/products/cloud-infrastructure/vsphere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">VMware vSphere</a> is VMware’s virtualization platform, with VMware ESXi being its bare metal hypervisor. It offers advanced resource management and automation tools alongside a strong ecosystem of products, making it a top choice for high-performance virtualization. Thanks to its integration with VMware workstation products, ESXi is suitable for enterprise businesses seeking a powerful, scalable hypervisor.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Built on vSphere 9, the Express product supports environments of up to 128 cores and includes vMotion for live VM migration, high availability, distributed switching. Broadcom hasn’t published a price, but distributor TD SYNNEX lists vSphere Express at $9,600 for the one-year package of 128 cores.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Free (ESXi 8.0 Update 3e, single host, no vCenter or support). Paid editions are per-core subscriptions sold through resellers. vSphere Express lists at about $9,600/year for up to 128 cores. Larger bundles (vSphere Foundation and VMware Cloud Foundation) are quote-based.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-3-proxmox-ve-virtual-environment\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">3. Proxmox VE (Virtual Environment)</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"366\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_366,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"promox bare metal hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475063\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_366,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794585476\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_366,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_357,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_321,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_285,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_250,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_214,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_178,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_142,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_107,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_71,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585476/prod/hypervisor-8/hypervisor-8.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.proxmox.com/en/proxmox-ve\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Proxmox Virtual Environment</a> is an open source virtualization platform that integrates KVM and Linux Containers. This makes it versatile, as you can use containers or VMs based on your workload.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Proxmox Virtual Environment 9.2 was released May 21, 2026. It’s based on Debian 13.5 “Trixie” and features Linux kernel 7.0, and its headline feature is a Dynamic Load Balancer.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While basic setup is fairly straightforward, you may need in-depth technical expertise to make the most of its advanced features. Extensive documentation and active community support make it a strong choice for organizations seeking a cost-effective and flexible virtualization platform.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Free, with optional paid support</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-4-microsoft-hyper-v\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">4. Microsoft Hyper-V</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/virtualization/hyper-v-on-windows/about/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Microsoft Hyper-V</a> is a bare metal hypervisor that comes with Windows Server, offering seamless integrations with Microsoft products. It also supports other operating systems like Linux and FreeBSD.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Hyper-V may have fewer features compared to some of its competitors, but its ease of use and integration with the Microsoft ecosystem make it a strong contender.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Included with Windows Server 2025 licensing. Standard edition starts at about $1,176 per 16 cores and covers two Windows Server VMs. Datacenter edition starts at about $6,771 per 16 cores and covers unlimited Windows Server VMs.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-5-kernel-based-virtual-machine-kvm\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">5. Kernel-Based Virtual Machine (KVM)</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://linux-kvm.org/page/Main_Page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Kernel-Based Virtual Machine</a>, also known as KVM, is an open source hypervisor integrated directly into the Linux kernel. It provides high performance and great scalability.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a cloud-ready solution, KVM supports diverse workloads and adapts to various environments. It powers platforms like Proxmox VE and OpenShift Virtualization and works with management tools like OpenStack. However, it requires a great deal of Linux expertise, which could be a barrier for beginners.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price: </strong>Free</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-6-red-hat-openshift-virtualization\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">6. Red Hat OpenShift Virtualization</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"362\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_362,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"red hat bare metal hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475060\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_362,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794584791\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_362,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_353,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_318,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_282,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_247,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_212,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_176,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_141,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_106,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_70,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584791/prod/hypervisor-4/hypervisor-4.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.redhat.com/en/technologies/cloud-computing/openshift/virtualization\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Red Hat OpenShift Virtualization</a> runs virtual machines alongside containers on Red Hat OpenShift, using the KVM hypervisor. It has replaced Red Hat Virtualization as Red Hat’s main virtualization platform. Teams that only need VMs can buy OpenShift Virtualization Engine, a lower-cost edition limited to VM workloads.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It’s a good fit for organizations moving off VMware or Red Hat Virtualization, especially if they plan to modernize applications later. The trade-off is complexity. OpenShift is a Kubernetes platform, so teams without Kubernetes experience should expect a learning curve.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Annual subscription per bare-metal server, sold through Red Hat or authorized partners.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-7-xenserver-formerly-citrix-hypervisor\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">7. XenServer (formerly Citrix Hypervisor)</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"335\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_335,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"xenserver bare metal hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475061\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_335,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794584998\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_335,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_327,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_294,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_261,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_229,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_196,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_163,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_130,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_98,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_65,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794584998/prod/hypervisor-5/hypervisor-5.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You probably know this bare metal hypervisor as <a href=\"https://www.xenserver.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">XenServer</a>. Citrix offers powerful enterprise virtualization software renowned for its high performance and scalability. It boasts a rich feature set, including live migration, comprehensive management tools, and the ability to handle demanding workloads.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Setting it up can be a complex process, and the high licensing fees can be a burden for some businesses. But it’s an excellent choice for businesses that need a reliable hypervisor that integrates with Citrix products.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price: </strong>Paid Premium Edition licensed per CPU socket. A free 90-day Trial Edition includes Premium features on up to three hosts.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-8-xen-project-xcp-ng\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">8. Xen Project/XCP-ng</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"431\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_431,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"xcp-ng bare metal hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475062\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_431,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794585205\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_431,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_420,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_378,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_336,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_294,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_252,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_210,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_168,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_126,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_84,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585205/prod/hypervisor-6/hypervisor-6.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Xen Project is an open source hypervisor that started at the University of Cambridge and is now maintained by the Linux Foundation. <a href=\"https://xcp-ng.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">XCP-ng</a> is a free, community-driven platform built on it. It was forked from Citrix XenServer in 2018, after Citrix stopped offering XenServer free and open source.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">As a community-driven, open source solution, XCP-ng is one of the best free bare metal hypervisors, making it an attractive option for organizations seeking cost-effective virtualization. However, setting it up requires technical expertise, and it may lack some advanced features found in other hypervisors.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">XCP-ng 8.3 LTS was released on 2024-10-07 and will be supported until 2028-11-30 to make way for the next major release, XCP-ng 9.0. It’s developed by Vates, with Xen Orchestra as the management layer.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Free</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-9-sangfor-asv\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">9. Sangfor aSV</h3>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"351\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_351,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"sangfor bare metal hypervisor\" class=\"wp-post-352715 wp-image-475066\" data-public-id=\"prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_351,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1794585926\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_351,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_342,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_308,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_274,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_239,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_205,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_171,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_137,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_102,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_68,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1794585926/prod/hypervisor-9/hypervisor-9.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.sangfor.com/cloud-and-infrastructure/products/asv-hypervisor-server-virtualization\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sangfor aSV Hypervisor</a> is a type-1, KVM-based bare metal hypervisor that forms the compute layer of Sangfor’s Hyper-Converged Infrastructure platform. It installs directly on x86 servers from vendors such as Dell, HPE, and Lenovo and is positioned as an enterprise alternative to VMware vSphere. Sangfor Cloud Platform, the product family that includes aSV, holds a 4.7/5 rating on G2 across 94 reviews.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">aSV is designed to make leaving VMware easier. It includes a CLI with 230+ PowerCLI-equivalent commands and a vCenter-like console, so VMware admins can keep familiar workflows, and automated VMDK conversion helps move existing VMs over. Networking, storage, and security (including micro-segmentation, WAF, and IPS) come in the same license instead of as separate add-ons like NSX and vSAN. For availability, it offers proactive HA 2.0, which flags likely node or VM failures before they cause downtime, and live migration with under 3 seconds of performance impact. It also connects to external iSCSI, FC, NFS, and CIFS storage. However, it’s proprietary with no free edition, its community and ecosystem are smaller than VMware’s, and pricing is only available by quote.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Quote-based (perpetual or subscription licensing).</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-10-oracle-linux-virtualization-manager\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">10. Oracle Linux Virtualization Manager</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.oracle.com/virtualization/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Oracle Linux Virtualization Manager</a> (OLVM) is Oracle’s current server virtualization platform and the successor to Oracle VM. It uses the KVM hypervisor on Oracle Linux hosts and is built on the open source oVirt project. It’s a natural choice for teams running Oracle databases and applications.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">OLVM handles clustering, live migration, snapshots, and monitoring from a web-based console. The software itself is free. However, it’s most at home in Oracle-centric environments, and teams without Linux or Oracle experience may find it harder to manage than more turnkey options.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Price:</strong> Free to download and use. Oracle support requires Oracle Linux Premier Support, starting at $1,399 per CPU pair per year.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Common use cases for bare metal hypervisors</h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Enterprise use (IoT, edge computing, DevOps)</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In the enterprise world, where efficiency and performance are paramount, bare metal hypervisors are increasingly being used in environments that require low latency and high scalability.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For instance, the <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/iot-cloud/\">Internet of Things</a> (IoT) and edge computing often involve deploying a large number of devices and services across different locations. By using bare metal virtualization, enterprises can manage their infrastructure more efficiently, ensure high availability, and minimize <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/server-downtime/\">downtime</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In DevOps environments, bare metal hypervisors provide the necessary resources for fast and reliable application development and testing. They allow teams to spin up virtual machines quickly for testing, staging, and production, all while ensuring that hardware resources are allocated efficiently.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">High-performance computing (AI/ML, research, etc.)</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal hypervisors are widely used in <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/high-performance-cloud-computing/\">high-performance computing</a> (HPC) environments, where the need for computational power is immense. Whether it&#8217;s <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/ai-for-beginners/\">Artificial Intelligence</a> (AI), Machine Learning (ML), data science, or scientific research, bare metal virtualization ensures that applications get the full benefit of the server’s resources.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">AI/ML workloads, in particular, require massive processing power, especially when working with large datasets or running complex algorithms. Bare metal hypervisors enable researchers and organizations to run these workloads in isolated environments while still maximizing the use of the underlying hardware. The direct access to physical resources means faster computations and more efficient use of the server.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Real-time processing (gaming, media, and streaming)</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For applications that require real-time processing – like online gaming, media production, and live streaming – bare metal hypervisors provide the speed and reliability necessary to avoid lag or downtime.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In gaming, for example, bare metal hypervisors can run game servers with the low latency needed for smooth player experiences. Similarly, media and streaming services require rapid processing and delivery of content to viewers without delays, making bare metal virtualization an excellent choice for these industries.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bare metal virtualization ensures that your virtual machines have access to dedicated hardware resources, allowing for optimal performance and seamless processing of real-time data streams.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Disaster recovery and business continuity</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One of the most critical aspects of running any enterprise is ensuring that your business can continue to function during a crisis. Bare metal hypervisors are crucial in <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/server-disaster-recovery/\">disaster recovery</a> plans, as they allow for high availability, fault tolerance, and easy replication of environments. In the event of a failure, virtual machines can quickly be moved to other physical servers, ensuring that operations continue with minimal disruption.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With bare metal virtualization, you can also implement high-availability setups, <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/load-balancing/\">load balancing</a>, and automated failover to make sure that your critical services are always up and running. The ability to restore virtual machines quickly and efficiently helps ensure business continuity even during the most challenging situations.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Regulated industries (finance, healthcare, etc.)</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Industries like finance, healthcare, and government have stringent regulations around data security, privacy, and compliance. Bare metal hypervisors are particularly well-suited for these environments because they offer enhanced security and control over the hardware. In regulated industries, keeping data isolated and secure is paramount, and bare metal virtualization provides that isolation while allowing organizations to run multiple virtual machines on the same physical server.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For example, in the healthcare industry, where patient data must be securely managed according to regulations like <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/what-is-hipaa-compliance/\">HIPAA</a>, bare metal hypervisors ensure that sensitive data remains isolated and protected. Similarly, financial institutions benefit from the strong security capabilities of bare metal virtualization, which helps protect sensitive financial data and ensures compliance with industry regulations.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to choose the best bare metal hypervisor</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With several high-performance hypervisors on the market, you’re probably wondering how to narrow down the list. Use this guide to select the best bare metal hypervisor for your needs:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Performance requirements</strong> – Evaluate your application’s performance demands. High-performance workloads require hypervisors with great resource management.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Scalability</strong> – If your business is rapidly growing, you need a hypervisor that can scale with you.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Cost considerations</strong> – Weigh your budget against your feature requirements. Luckily, some bare metal hypervisors are free to use.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Usability</strong> – How easy is it to set up and use the hypervisor? How skilled is your technical team? User-friendly interfaces and advanced management tools can save time and effort.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Security track record</strong> – Look at the hypervisor’s security history. Is it well-built or prone to breaches? Bare metal hypervisors inherently offer better isolation, but a solution with built-in security features is a superior choice.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Compatibility</strong> – Verify compatibility with your existing tech stack. If you’re already using a company’s suite of products (e.g., VMware, Oracle, or Red Hat), consider using its hypervisor for better compatibility.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Support and community resources</strong> – Ensure the company has a reliable support team. An active community is a plus. A strong support network can be a great help when troubleshooting.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-getting-started-with-a-bare-metal-hypervisor\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Getting started with a bare metal hypervisor</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Your application workloads require a bare metal hypervisor that’s able to provide great performance, scalability, and security. Selecting the right one is crucial.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">All the bare metal hypervisors we’ve discussed offer full control over resources and better efficiency for your most critical applications. But which one is best for your business? If you’re not sure, <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/on-premises-to-cloud-migration/\">combine the flexibility of the cloud</a> with the power of bare metal servers.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">That’s what <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/cloud-hosting/dedicated-cloud-server/\">Liquid Web cloud metal</a> offers. You’ll get a high-availability server customized to meet your requirements – you choose the CPU model you want. Take advantage of our bare metal cloud hosting today, or <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/contact-us/\">reach out</a> to discuss which plan would best suit your needs!</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https://www.servers.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shop bare metal on Servers.com</a></div>\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>Originally posted December 2024; updated November 2026</em></p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/bare-metal-hypervisors/\">10 best bare metal hypervisors for more powerful workflows</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:1;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:60:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:49:\"Recap and live replay: Nexcess Software Town Hall\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:97:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/webinar-recap-live-replay-nexcess-software-town-hall-october-2026/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:8:\"Software\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:23:\"Software Virtual Events\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=468091\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:569:\"<p>Thanks to everyone who joined us for the October 14 town hall. This recap follows the session order, for anyone who couldn’t make it. You can also watch the full recording.&#160; Looking for details on our new package and pricing? Ways to stay in touch You’ll see more of our product managers from here on,...</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/webinar-recap-live-replay-nexcess-software-town-hall-october-2026/\">Recap and live replay: Nexcess Software Town Hall</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Morgan Hugoboom\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:21674:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Thanks to everyone who joined us for the October 14 town hall. This recap follows the session order, for anyone who couldn’t make it. You can also <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-town-hall\">watch the full recording.</a>&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id468091_1414da-b0 alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column468091_5197fa-44\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Looking for details on our new package and pricing?</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"#changes-to-software\">Go here</a> for a detailed discussion from the call</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Or, more info can be found <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-packages\">here</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-yoast-seo-table-of-contents yoast-table-of-contents\"><h2>Table of contents</h2><ul><li><a href=\"#h-ways-to-stay-in-touch\" data-level=\"3\">Ways to stay in touch</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-where-we-ve-come-from\" data-level=\"3\">Where we’ve come from</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-new-in-kadence-the-design-system\" data-level=\"3\">New in Kadence: the design system</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-new-in-give-standalone-and-embeddable-forms\" data-level=\"3\">New in Give: standalone and embeddable forms</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-in-progress-learndash-certificates\" data-level=\"3\">In progress: LearnDash certificates</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-in-progress-the-events-calendar\" data-level=\"3\">In progress: The Events Calendar</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-updated-nexcess-branding-in-the-plugins\" data-level=\"3\">Updated Nexcess branding in the plugins</a></li><li><a href=\"#changes-to-software\" data-level=\"3\">Changes to software plans</a></li><li><a href=\"#h-what-happens-next\" data-level=\"3\">What happens next</a></li></ul></div>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-ways-to-stay-in-touch\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong><strong>Ways to stay in touch</strong></strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You’ll see more of our product managers from here on, sharing work in progress, asking for your feedback, and keeping you posted on what’s next.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Here are the places to find us right now:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Feature boards: they’re back, with one for each product: <a href=\"https://kadence.nolt.io/\">Kadence</a>, <a href=\"https://learndash.nolt.io/\">LearnDash</a>, <a href=\"https://tec.nolt.io/\">The Events Calendar</a>, and <a href=\"https://give.nolt.io/\">Give</a>.&nbsp;</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Support and account services: can help with account or billing questions. Submit a ticket from <a href=\"http://www.software.liquidweb.com\">your portal</a>.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Newsletter: software news and product updates. <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-newsletter-signup\">Sign up here</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-where-we-ve-come-from\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong><strong>Where we’ve come from</strong></strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Customers often ask what Liquid Web and Nexcess are, so the session included a timeline of how the company got here:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-full is-resized\"><img width=\"1058\" height=\"313\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1058,h_313,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-468091 wp-image-468097\" style=\"aspect-ratio:3.3777777777777778;width:811px;height:auto\" data-public-id=\"prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png\" data-format=\"png\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1792185884\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1058,h_313,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 1058w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_295,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_266,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_236,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_207,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_177,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_147,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_118,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_88,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_59,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1792185884/prod/image_46809797856/image_46809797856.png?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1058px) 100vw, 1058px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Kadence, Give, LearnDash, and The Events Calendar have been part of the Liquid Web family for several years. Together, they sit within a larger group of products and services for building and running websites, with a history going back 30 years.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-new-in-kadence-the-design-system\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong><strong>New in Kadence: the design system</strong></strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We released the Kadence Blocks design system on October 14. When you update Kadence Blocks, you’ll find a new style library in the WordPress admin.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You set base styles once, including colors, typography, shadows, border radius and padding. Presets for buttons, advanced images, and other blocks draw from your style, so when you change it, every block that uses it updates it across your site.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In the session, Jehrome demonstrated these new functionalities by walking us through a demo site he’d built. He changed a heading color and a button color in two edits, without searching through every block. <em>(You can see the demo in the</em> <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-town-hall\"><em>Town Hall replay</em></a><em>.)</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The design system is most useful if you build multiple sites or work with other designers, because everyone draws from the same library. That keeps your sites consistent and makes changes faster.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">More block presets are on the way, and the ones available now are the ones users rely on most.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Questions from the session:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Global styles:</strong> You can set collections of styles and update them across a site today, using the design system.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Full site editing:</strong> We’re building Kadence full site editing to work alongside the design system, so you’ll be able to use block presets in the full site editor. The Kadence <a href=\"https://kadence.nolt.io/\">feature board</a> is the best place to follow progress.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Import and export:</strong> You can’t import or export presets yet, and they don’t use JSON. Jehrome agreed the idea makes sense, and will take it to the team.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> It remains a priority. We continue to work with Equalize Digital, who audit our updates, and we patch the issues they find.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>AI and MCP access:</strong> Many of you already build with Kadence and AI, and we’ve enjoyed seeing that work in the community. For now, the Kadence team is focused on full site editing and the design system, which will put Kadence in a better position to work with AI tools. The goal is for you to work with whichever AI tool you prefer. We don’t plan to add new chat windows to the site builder, and we’ll only add AI where it’s useful.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-new-in-give-standalone-and-embeddable-forms\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong><strong>New in Give: standalone and embeddable forms</strong></strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We also released standalone forms and embeddable forms on October 14, and Jehrome walked through both in test mode.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A year ago, a Give form could only exist inside a campaign. Standalone forms bring back the option to create a form on its own.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Embeddable forms let you copy a script from a form’s external website option and paste it into any site, whether it’s built on Shopify, Squarespace, Wix or WordPress. The full donation flow then runs on that site with no extra setup, which helps if you manage donation forms across many sites.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-in-progress-learndash-certificates\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>In progress: LearnDash certificates</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Jehrome showed an early version of a new certificate builder.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The current builder is dated and clunky, so the new one is meant to fix that. It uses drag and drop and snaps items to a grid, so lining things up is easy. It’s still early, so stay tuned for more news and a demo closer to release.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>Not signed up for our newsletter yet?</em> <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-newsletter-signup\"><em>Subscribe here</em></a> <em>for updates.</em></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Certificates will also become verifiable. Learners will be able to share a link showing where the certificate came from and which site issued it, which helps with policy review, employee training, and compliance records.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-in-progress-the-events-calendar\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>In progress: The Events Calendar</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Events Calendar team is updating the older parts of the interface. The current focus is on recurring events with tickets. An attendee asked about accessibility issues flagged in audits, and Jehrome will take that question to TEC product manager.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-updated-nexcess-branding-in-the-plugins\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Updated Nexcess branding in the plugins</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Licensing inside each plugin now carries Nexcess branding, and the portal will follow. We’re also redesigning the portal to make it easier to manage licenses, activate sites, and handle subscriptions.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"changes-to-software\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Changes to software plans</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Starting with renewals on or after November 16, Kadence, Give, LearnDash, and The Events Calendar come together as one software package: the <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-packages\"><strong>Nexcess plugin stack</strong></a>.</p>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id468091_ec8358-5d alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column468091_521118-7a\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>NOTE:</strong> Some subscriptions, including Kadence plans, are not moving to the new software stack. The full list is under “What isn’t changing” in this section.&nbsp;</p>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Why we’re doing it</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">By bringing our software solutions into one package, there’s one version of each product to build on. That means updates and fixes (including vulnerability patches) can reach you sooner, more of our time goes into improving the products you use, and because everyone is on the same version, our support team can find and solve issues faster.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Earlier this year, we folded more than ten LearnDash add-ons into the core LearnDash product. The package follows the same idea: one license covers everything, and you install only what you need.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What’s included</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Every plan includes all four products with every premium feature, on every site in your plan. There are no tiers to move through, and you install only the products you need, when you need them. Billing, invoices, and downloads are all in one account, and our team supports all four products. Kadence Security is sold separately.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Each product includes:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Kadence</strong> (design, theme, blocks): Kadence Theme Pro, Kadence Blocks Pro, and the marketing suite with pop-ups, slide-ins, and sale banners.&nbsp;</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>LearnDash</strong> (courses and memberships): Build courses with a drag-and-drop builder with eight quiz question types, and sell memberships, subscriptions, and course bundles through MemberDash. You can sell group seats to organizations, add more than one instructor to a course, track enrollment, completion, and quiz results with ProPanel reporting and a gradebook, and award certificates and achievements.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>The Events Calendar</strong> (events and ticketing): Sell tickets with QR check-in and 0% ticket commission, and set up assigned seating with a seating map builder. Promote events with scheduled and triggered email campaigns, run virtual and hybrid events, and accept community event submissions.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Give</strong> (fundraising): Accept recurring donations, let donors cover processing fees, and run peer-to-peer fundraising, tribute gifts, and funds and designations. Gift Aid and currency switching support international donors, and Give connects to Mailchimp, Salesforce, Zapier, and more.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With all four in one package, you can use them together:&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>If you sell courses, you can run live workshops and sell seats with The Events Calendar, take donations with Give, and redesign your site in Kadence.&nbsp;</li>\n\n\n\n<li>If you run a nonprofit, you can sell gala tickets with assigned seating, train volunteers in LearnDash, and build campaign pages with Kadence.&nbsp;</li>\n\n\n\n<li>If you build client sites, every site in your plan gets courses, events, fundraising, and store tools, with no extra licenses.&nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h4 id=\"h-what-isn-t-changing\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>What isn’t changing</strong></h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">These subscriptions stay as they are:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Kadence subscriptions, including lifetime, annual, and unlimited sites</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Iconic products</li>\n\n\n\n<li>The LearnDash app</li>\n\n\n\n<li>LearnDash add-ons we acquired from WisdmLabs</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Products formerly known as Solid, such as Kadence Security</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Restrict Content Pro</li>\n\n\n\n<li>StellarSites</li>\n\n\n\n<li>LearnDash Cloud and LearnDash Domains</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Any unlimited site license, lifetime license, or The Events Calendar Multisite license (the specific product built for WordPress Multisite)</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Accounts with more than 100 active licenses</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Customers who hold one of these licenses alongside something that is converting will see a change <strong>only to the converting one</strong>.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For example, if you have a Kadence lifetime license and a LearnDash subscription, your Kadence license stays as it is and LearnDash moves to the package.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you have a Kadence Ultimate lifetime plan plus LearnDash and The Events Calendar, your Kadence plan stays the same, and LearnDash and The Events Calendar combine into one package subscription.</p>\n\n\n\n<h4 id=\"h-plans-and-pricing\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Plans and pricing</strong></h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Plans are priced by the number of sites. Every plan renews yearly and includes all four products.</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\"><strong>Plan</strong></th><th class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\"><strong>Price per year</strong></th><th class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\"><strong>Cost per site</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">1 site</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$350</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$350</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">Up to 10 sites</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$599</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$59.90</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">Up to 25 sites</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$799</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$31.96</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">Up to 50 sites</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$899</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$17.98</td></tr><tr><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">Up to 100 sites</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$999</td><td class=\"has-text-align-center\" data-align=\"center\">$9.99</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><br>A few notes on how this works:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Agency pricing is back for up to 100 sites. The 100-site plan comes to as low as $9.99 per site.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>To add sites, you move up a plan instead of buying new licenses.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Accounts with more than 100 sites get a custom plan through our <a href=\"https://www.nexcess.com/contact/\">account management team</a>.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Kadence Security is sold separately. If you have it today, it stays on its own plan and renews as it does now.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Before you renew, you’ll get an email with your plan, number of sites, price, and renewal date. You can also find your renewal date and site count in your account.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What you need to do</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You don’t need to do anything right now. Your current plans keep working until it renews, your renewal dates stay the same, and your license keys don’t change. You’ll get the usual 30 days’ notice before you renew.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Timeline.</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Week of October 12: emails go to customers who renew between November 16 and December 1.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Week of October 19: emails go to customers who renew after December 1, plus a newsletter and a new customer resources page.&nbsp;</li>\n\n\n\n<li>November 3: You can log into the portal to see which plan you’ll move to and your site count.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>November 16: the first renewals into the stack begin.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Over the next couple of weeks, our customer success and billing teams will also email customers about what applies to their accounts.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>If you have several plans on different renewal dates</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The first renewal to come up becomes the stack. The other subscriptions are canceled when the stack is applied, leaving one subscription to manage.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Customers who prefer not to renew into the stack</strong> can cancel any time before the renewal date.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For more information on the new stack and what it means for you, <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-packages\">check out this guide</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-what-happens-next\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>What happens next</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Our town hall was a packed session, with several new releases and plenty of thoughtful questions.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To catch the next town hall and stay current on product news, watch for our newsletter. Anyone who hasn’t subscribed yet can <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-newsletter-signup\">sign up here</a>.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To tell us what features and functionalities you need, post on the feature boards: <a href=\"https://kadence.nolt.io/\">Kadence</a>, <a href=\"https://learndash.nolt.io/\">LearnDash</a>, <a href=\"https://tec.nolt.io/\">The Events Calendar</a>, and <a href=\"https://give.nolt.io/\">Give</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Thanks again to everyone who spent an hour with us. To catch the full replay, <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-town-hall\">watch here</a>.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/webinar-recap-live-replay-nexcess-software-town-hall-october-2026/\">Recap and live replay: Nexcess Software Town Hall</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:2;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:63:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:96:\"Software webinar recap: what’s coming across Kadence, LearnDash, The Events Calendar, and Give\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:117:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/webinar-recap-whats-coming-across-kadence-learndash-the-events-calendar-and-give-2026/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:3:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:17:\"Product Tutorials\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:8:\"Software\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:23:\"Software Virtual Events\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=467141\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:674:\"<p>We just wrapped the first round of a new webinar series covering Kadence, LearnDash, The Events Calendar, and Give. Every six weeks, we’re talking through best practices, what&#8217;s new, and tips for getting more out of the tools you already use. This first round was four separate sessions, one for each product. Going forward, we&#8217;re...</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/webinar-recap-whats-coming-across-kadence-learndash-the-events-calendar-and-give-2026/\">Software webinar recap: what&#8217;s coming across Kadence, LearnDash, The Events Calendar, and Give</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Morgan Hugoboom\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:7150:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We just wrapped the first round of a new webinar series covering <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/software/kadence/\">Kadence</a>, <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/software/learndash/\">LearnDash</a>, <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/software/the-events-calendar/\">The Events Calendar</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/software/give/\">Give</a>. Every six weeks, we’re talking through best practices, what&#8217;s new, and tips for getting more out of the tools you already use.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This first round was four separate sessions, one for each product. Going forward, we&#8217;re combining them into a single session that covers all four products at once.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you missed any of the sessions, or want more detail than we had room for, here&#8217;s the full recap.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-shared-updates-across-all-four-products\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Shared updates across all four products</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A few updates apply to every product:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Roadmaps are public again.</strong> You&#8217;ll be able to see what we’ve done, what we’re working on, and where we’re going next.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Feature requests are coming back too.</strong> Submit ideas and upvote what matters to you.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>We&#8217;re connecting our products to each other.</strong> If something works well in one product, we&#8217;re working to bring it to the others instead of building it from scratch each time.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-more-ways-we-re-staying-in-touch\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>More ways we&#8217;re staying in touch</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We&#8217;re rolling out a few new ways to keep you in the loop:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>These webinar sessions will run every six weeks</li>\n\n\n\n<li>We’ll also publish a blog recap for anyone who can&#8217;t join a webinar live</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Feature request boards are coming back for all four products</li>\n\n\n\n<li>We&#8217;re building a dedicated page to house the latest product updates</li>\n\n\n\n<li>…and a lot more</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Our newsletter is the easiest way to catch all of this in one place and to hear about each new communication channel we launch.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-newsletter-signup\">Sign up here</a> to get the newsletter delivered right to your inbox.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-kadence\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Kadence</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What we&#8217;re focused on:</strong> building the best WordPress theme and block experience possible. Kadence remains the most accessible WordPress theme available, independently verified, and we&#8217;re continuing to invest here as accessibility standards keep evolving.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>A full site editor is in active development, giving you centralized control over things like border radius, colors, fonts, and button styles applied consistently across your whole site</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Coming soon: <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/software/kadence/blocks/\">Kadence Blocks</a> will be getting a Design System. You’ll be able to configure style and block presets to use across your site, making it easy to implement site-wide style changes in a few clicks.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>We&#8217;ve added support for the WordPress Abilities API and MCP, so you can connect your Kadence site to Claude or another AI tool and build with it directly</li>\n\n\n\n<li>We launched a new starter template called Gilbert, built for agencies and freelancers who want a service and portfolio focused site</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-learndash\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>LearnDash</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What we&#8217;re focused on:</strong> making courses built in LearnDash carry real, verifiable weight, and giving course creators more ways to connect with the people taking their courses.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Certificates are built to be used beyond LearnDash, so learners can add them to a resume or LinkedIn profile</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A new certificate builder is coming, with precise drag and drop placement, snap to grid alignment, and the ability to share and verify certificates publicly</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Certificates will be stored by completion date, opening the door to recertification, like annual compliance training, without duplicating an entire course</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Community-building features are on the way, alongside the course tools you already rely on</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-the-events-calendar\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>The Events Calendar</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What we&#8217;re focused on:</strong> making it faster and easier to set up and run an event, without needing a developer to get there.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Recurring events with tickets is getting a much needed overhaul. Today, setting up tickets for a recurring event means converting it to a single event and attaching a ticket manually, event by event. We&#8217;re replacing that with a default ticket that applies across all instances, with the option to override price or quantity for any date</li>\n\n\n\n<li>We&#8217;re building no-code controls so you can customize the look of your events without needing a developer</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-give\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Give</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What we&#8217;re focused on:</strong> making Give flexible enough to support the many different ways nonprofits actually raise money and run their organizations, wherever their donors are.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Embeddable forms are coming. You&#8217;ll be able to build a donation form in Give and place it on any website, not just WordPress. The entire donation happens right there in the embed, with no redirect, and every donation flows straight into your Give account</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Donation forms just got more flexible: build a form and drop it on a page, no extra layer required</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Ticketing in Give is getting a major upgrade by connecting to our existing Events Calendar ticketing tools, bringing waitlists, check-in apps, and custom fields along with it</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We loved getting to show you what we&#8217;re working on, and we can&#8217;t wait to see you <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-town-hall\">at the next webinar session in a few weeks</a>. Keep an eye on your inbox for the invite.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Want more updates? <a href=\"https://explore.nexcess.com/software-newsletter-signup\">Sign up for our newsletter</a>.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/webinar-recap-whats-coming-across-kadence-learndash-the-events-calendar-and-give-2026/\">Software webinar recap: what&#8217;s coming across Kadence, LearnDash, The Events Calendar, and Give</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:3;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:69:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:56:\"WordPress client dashboard tips every agency should know\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:63:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/wordpress-client-dashboard-tips/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:5:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:29:\"Agency Operations / WordPress\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:12:\"agency tools\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:17:\"client management\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11:\"white-label\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:4;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:19:\"WordPress dashboard\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466756\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:398:\"<p>A confusing WP-Admin screen means more support tickets, not fewer. Here\'s how agencies simplify, white-label, and customize the WordPress dashboard clients actually see.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/wordpress-client-dashboard-tips/\">WordPress client dashboard tips every agency should know</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9800:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When your agency hands off a new WordPress site, the client starts logging into WP-Admin. WordPress ships that screen the same way for everybody, so unless you change it, what you delivered is the dashboard built for developers. A WordPress client dashboard is what WP-Admin looks like once you have set what each person can reach, cleared away what they do not need, and put branding on it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Doing that gets you: a client who can find the job they logged in to do, a client who cannot reach the settings that break a site, and a handover that looks finished. Below is how to set one up, in the order that works. Ask what the client will be doing, set their role, clear and brand their screens, then keep the result as the starting point for your next build.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-ask-what-the-client-will-be-doing-on-the-site\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ask what the client will be doing on the site</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Asking what the client will be doing on the site gives you the list everything else is built from. For most clients it comes to three or four jobs: publish a post, change some copy, upload images, check orders. Ask the people who will be logging in.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">That list tells you which role each person needs and which links belong on their first screen. It also stops you removing something they turn out to use, which makes their job harder and puts them back in your inbox.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-set-the-right-role-for-each-person\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Set the right role for each person</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Setting the right role for each person is how you control what they can reach. WordPress comes with five roles built in, and one of them covers most clients with no extra work.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Administrator</strong> can do everything, including installing plugins, switching themes and editing site settings.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Editor</strong> can write, edit, publish and delete any post or page, and manage media and comments.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Author</strong> can publish and manage only their own posts.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Contributor</strong> can write posts but cannot publish them.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Subscriber</strong> can manage their own profile and nothing else.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Editor is the one most clients need. It covers a typical content list while keeping the person out of plugins, themes and settings, which are places where a wrong click takes a site down.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When Editor does not cover something a client has to do, such as handling WooCommerce orders, add that permission to a copy of Editor instead of taking permissions away from Administrator. PublishPress Capabilities and User Role Editor both do this. Building up from Editor means the only permissions switched on are ones you chose, while stripping down from Administrator means remembering everything you meant to remove.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You will also see advice about hiding menus from clients. Hiding tidies the screen and it is not a permission, because the page behind a hidden menu still loads for anyone who has the URL. Use roles for what people are allowed to do and menu hiding for what they have to look at.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-clear-the-dashboard-and-brand-the-login-screen\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Clear the dashboard and brand the login screen</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Clearing the dashboard and branding the login screen fixes the two screens a client sees most.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The dashboard is what loads straight after login, and WordPress fills it with a news feed, an activity panel and whatever your plugins have added. None of it relates to the list you wrote, so the client scans past all of it every time. Clear it and put up one panel with links to their three or four jobs and a line saying how to reach you, so they land where they are going. White Label CMS does this without any code. Hide plugin update and licence notices from non-admin roles while you are there, because a client who sees one either ignores it or emails you about work you already handle.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The login screen is about how finished the work looks. A client seeing a default WordPress logo every week is being reminded whose software it is rather than whose site it is. White Label CMS covers the login logo and background, the admin bar, the menus and the footer, and Branda does the same while also rebranding system emails.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-save-it-as-the-starting-point-for-your-next-build\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Save it as the starting point for your next build</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Saving it as the starting point for your next build is what stops this being a job you redo constantly. What each client needs to do varies from site to site. The setup around it mostly does not, because the branding, the cleared widgets, the hidden notices and the standard Editor role are the same on every site you deliver.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Set those up once on an install you keep, and clone that install for new builds. The part that genuinely varies, which is usually one custom role and the links on the welcome panel, is then the only piece you build fresh each time.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id466756_2f4162-1c alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column466756_4f2812-86\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-accordion alignnone accordion\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-wrap kt-accordion-id466756_a3eac2-1d kt-accordion-has-26-panes kt-active-pane-0 kt-accordion-block kt-pane-header-alignment-left kt-accodion-icon-style-xclose kt-accodion-icon-side-right\" style=\"max-width:none\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-inner-wrap\" data-allow-multiple-open=\"false\" data-start-open=\"none\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-1 kt-pane466756_1dc93d-c2\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Do I need a paid plugin to do this?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No. The five built-in roles handle permissions with no plugin at all, and the free version of White Label CMS covers the login screen, the dashboard panels and the admin menus. Paid tools add things like rebranded system emails and custom CSS.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-2 kt-pane466756_ca7f49-bb\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">What if a client insists on Administrator access?<br></span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading466756_6e54d4-0b wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading466756_6e54d4-0b\">It is their site, so that is their decision. Explain what Administrator can reach, keep your own separate Administrator account so you never lose access, and put an activity logging plugin on the site, since an Administrator can change anything and you will want a record of what changed.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-3 kt-pane466756_a659e9-12\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">How often should I change my starting install?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whenever the same question reaches you from a second client. A repeated question usually points at a menu that confuses people, a notice that should have been hidden, or a link missing from the welcome panel, and changing the base means the next site ships with the fix already in it.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Agency hosting built for a portfolio</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Liquid Web&#8217;s managed WordPress hosting is built for agencies running client sites in volume, with staging environments for building and testing your base install before it goes near a client.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We build WordPress software too. LearnDash, Kadence, Give and The Events Calendar are Nexcess products, so the people you reach when a client site misbehaves know both the hosting and the plugins on it.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/wordpress-client-dashboard-tips/\">WordPress client dashboard tips every agency should know</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:4;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:69:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:47:\"Signs you’ve outgrown a SaaS website platform\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:61:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/outgrow-saas-website-platform/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:5:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:30:\"Ecommerce / Platform Migration\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:16:\"ecommerce growth\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:18:\"platform migration\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:14:\"SaaS platforms\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:4;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:7:\"Shopify\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466754\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:349:\"<p>Rate hikes, API caps, forced categories — the warning signs a SaaS platform is now the ceiling on your growth, not the floor.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/outgrow-saas-website-platform/\">Signs you&#8217;ve outgrown a SaaS website platform</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:8163:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Plenty of stores open on a SaaS website platform, because you get a working shop, a checkout and payments on day one without hiring anybody. Outgrowing one has nothing to do with how big you get. It happens when costs and limits start appearing on your bill. Checking these four signs gives you the figure and the list you need to decide whether staying is worth it.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-four-signs-you-ve-outgrown-your-saas-website-platform\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Four signs you’ve outgrown your SaaS website platform</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The four signs you’ve outgrown your SaaS website platform each show up in a different part of the business: your fees, your app bill, your other systems, and your customer records.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Transaction fees.</strong> The platform takes a percentage of every order, which was a small amount at launch volume and is a larger one now, so the figure to look at is what it came to across last year.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Apps doing the platform&#8217;s job.</strong> You pay monthly for add-ons handling bundles, trade pricing, subscriptions or stock, and together they cost more than the plan they sit on.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Systems that will not talk to each other.</strong> Your accounting or stock system needs order data, the platform limits how much you can pull and how often, and somebody exports a spreadsheet every week instead.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Customer records you can’t access.</strong> You can see customers in the dashboard, but getting a full list with order history into your email tool takes a support ticket or another paid app.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-your-platform-costs-and-what-to-compare-it-to\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What your platform costs, and what to compare it to</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Working out what your platform costs takes about 20 minutes with your billing records. Add up last year&#8217;s subscription, the transaction fees on every order, and every app you pay for to make the store do its job, then divide that total by your revenue for the same year. The percentage you get is what your store costs to run before stock, wages, or marketing.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Do the same calculation for the year you launched. If the percentage has climbed while the platform stayed the same, it’s taking a larger share from your larger business.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On owned software the shape of the cost is different. Hosting and plugins are fixed monthly and annual amounts, so they stay the same through a strong quarter and a slow one, and the percentage drops as revenue grows. Payment processing still takes its cut, because that is your card processor and not your platform.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-short-term-fixes-and-what-they-leave-in-place\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Short-term fixes, and what they leave in place</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Short-term fixes lower what you’re paying without changing the thing that produced the bill.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Go through your app billing.</strong> Most stores are paying for add-ons nobody has opened in a year.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Check whether a higher plan tier lowers your transaction rate.</strong> At enough volume the saving on fees covers the price difference. You’ll still pay a percentage, just a smaller one.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Write down everything connected to your store.</strong> Accounting, stock, shipping, email. That list is what decides how much work a migration would be, so it is useful whether you go or stay.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id466754_e82824-93 alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column466754_785719-70\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-accordion alignnone accordion\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-wrap kt-accordion-id466754_807274-cf kt-accordion-has-26-panes kt-active-pane-0 kt-accordion-block kt-pane-header-alignment-left kt-accodion-icon-style-xclose kt-accodion-icon-side-right\" style=\"max-width:none\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-inner-wrap\" data-allow-multiple-open=\"false\" data-start-open=\"none\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-1 kt-pane466754_42dd91-f6\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Does outgrowing a SaaS platform mean my store has got too big for it?<br></span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No, and size is not the measure. A store on modest revenue with complicated products, trade pricing or a stock system to sync with hits the limits earlier than a bigger store selling twenty simple items.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-2 kt-pane466754_f296f9-6a\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Is moving off a SaaS platform a big project?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading466754_3780a3-f4 wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading466754_3780a3-f4\">It is a project, and its size depends on your integrations more than your product count. Products, customers and order history move with established tools, while anything connected to your store takes the planning, which is why that list is the first thing to make.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-3 kt-pane466754_0aa0c9-05\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Do I have to run servers myself if I leave?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No. Managed hosting means somebody else handles the server, the updates and the backups, while you own the store software and your records.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-where-this-leaves-you\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where this leaves you</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Work out your platform cost as a percentage of revenue for last year and for your launch year, and write down everything connected to your store. Those two things together tell you what the platform is costing you and what it would take to leave.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Nexcess runs managed WordPress and WooCommerce hosting, where the store software is open source and yours. Nobody takes a percentage of your orders, nobody caps how often you can pull your own data, and moving elsewhere later stays your decision rather than ours.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We build open source too. LearnDash, Kadence, Give and The Events Calendar are ours, released under the same license as WordPress, so the people running your hosting maintain it, too.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/outgrow-saas-website-platform/\">Signs you&#8217;ve outgrown a SaaS website platform</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:5;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:69:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:59:\"The benefits of open source software for a growing business\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:81:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/benefits-of-open-source-software-growing-business/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:5:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:21:\"Platform / Technology\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:17:\"business software\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:17:\"digital ownership\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11:\"Open source\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:4;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9:\"WordPress\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466752\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:384:\"<p>Open source software isn\'t just free — it\'s ownership. Here\'s why growing businesses across every model benefit from building on it.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/benefits-of-open-source-software-growing-business/\">The benefits of open source software for a growing business</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:8571:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When you choose the software your business runs on, you’re picking between two models. Either you hold a license to the software itself, or you rent access to somebody else&#8217;s and pay monthly for a product you can’t take with you. Open source software is the first kind, released under a license that lets anyone use it, look at how it works, change it, and pass it on. The examples here are WordPress, though the same logic applies to anything licensed the same way.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-you-get-when-software-is-open-source\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What you get when software is open source</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">What you get when software is open source is a set of rights written into its license. WordPress is released under the GNU General Public License version 2 or later, which gives anyone holding a copy the right to run it for any purpose, study how it works, change it, and pass it on with or without their changes. WordPress states that plugins and themes inherit the same license, so the tools built on top of it carry the same terms.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In practical terms, the company that made the software can’t revoke your right to keep running the copy you already have.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-four-advantages-of-open-source-software-as-you-grow\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Four advantages of open source software as you grow</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Four advantages of open source software show up as a business gets bigger, and each one comes from holding the license rather than renting access to somebody else&#8217;s product.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>The version you have stays yours.</strong> A company can change its pricing, alter its support terms, or charge differently for future versions, and open source licensing does not stop any of that. What it does stop is anyone taking away the copy you’re already running.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>You can move whenever you want to.</strong> The software is not tied to one supplier, so changing host means moving your files and your database and nothing else.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>You can hire almost anyone.</strong> WordPress runs 40.7% of all websites and 58.9% of sites whose content management system is known, according to <a href=\"https://w3techs.com/technologies/comparison/cm-wordpress\">W3Techs in August 2026</a>, so a large number of developers already know it without needing to learn your setup first.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>You can change what it does.</strong> If the software does not do something you need, you or somebody you pay can add it, without waiting for a feature to reach a roadmap.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-to-know-before-you-build-on-open-source-software\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to know before you build on open source software</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Before you build on open source software, four things are worth getting straight: </p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>It is not free to run.</strong> The license costs nothing. Hosting, plugins, themes, and somebody&#8217;s time to keep everything updated, all cost money. Paid plugins are not a contradiction, because you are buying the work and the support rather than permission.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Security depends on updates.</strong> The code is public, so flaws get found faster and are visible to everyone once they are. Sites that get compromised are almost always running versions that are out-of-date.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Nobody owes you support.</strong> Community forums answer when they answer. If your business can’t absorb a day of waiting, buy support from your host or from the companies making your plugins.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Plugins get abandoned.</strong> Anyone can publish one and walk away. Check when it was last updated, how many active installations it has, and whether a company or one individual maintains it. The WordPress plugin directory shows all three on every listing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id466752_060888-a5 alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column466752_65c162-70\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-accordion alignnone accordion\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-wrap kt-accordion-id466752_b7736d-f4 kt-accordion-has-26-panes kt-active-pane-0 kt-accordion-block kt-pane-header-alignment-left kt-accodion-icon-style-xclose kt-accodion-icon-side-right\" style=\"max-width:none\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-inner-wrap\" data-allow-multiple-open=\"false\" data-start-open=\"none\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-1 kt-pane466752_f57f04-b6\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Is open source software free?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The license is free and running the software is not. You pay for hosting, for most serious plugins, and for whoever keeps it updated. What you avoid is paying a company for permission to keep using what you built.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-2 kt-pane466752_32aca0-9a\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Is open source software secure enough for a business?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading466752_44b9dc-32 wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading466752_44b9dc-32\">Yes, provided it is kept updated. Managed WordPress hosting normally covers core updates and often plugin updates too, which takes the most neglected part of this off your hands, though what is included varies between hosts and plans, so check rather than assume.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-3 kt-pane466752_8bcc48-30\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Who is responsible when open source software breaks?<br></span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whoever you have a contract with, which is why most businesses buy managed hosting or paid plugin support. Nobody in the community owes you a reply, so a guaranteed response time has to come from a commercial relationship.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where this leaves you</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you are choosing what to build your business on, ask what happens to your site if the company behind the software changes its terms. With open source software you carry on running what you have, which is a large part of why growing businesses end up on WordPress.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Nexcess hosts open source software as its whole business. Your WordPress install, your plugins and your data stay yours, and moving them elsewhere stays your decision.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We also build it. LearnDash, Kadence, Give and The Events Calendar are ours, released under the same license as WordPress, so the people running your managed WordPress hosting maintain four of the tools you are likely running on your site.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/benefits-of-open-source-software-growing-business/\">The benefits of open source software for a growing business</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:6;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:69:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:51:\"LMS scalability: can your platform handle a launch?\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:75:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/lms-scalability-can-your-platform-handle-it/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:5:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:20:\"LMS / Online Courses\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9:\"learndash\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:3:\"LMS\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:16:\"online education\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:4;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11:\"Scalability\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466749\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:356:\"<p>Ten students or ten thousand — here\'s how to tell if your LMS can actually scale, and what breaks first when it can\'t.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/lms-scalability-can-your-platform-handle-it/\">LMS scalability: can your platform handle a launch?</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9406:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you sell courses in launches or run students through in cohorts, you need to consider what happens when a few hundred people sign in within the same hour and all start module one together. LMS scalability normally gets described as how many students a platform can support in total. This article shows you how to work out how many students will be using your site at the same moment, and what to do with that number once you have it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you sell evergreen courses, where people buy and start whenever they like, your enrollments arrive steadily and the load spreads across weeks. Most of what follows will not affect you.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-why-an-lms-puts-more-load-on-a-server-than-a-normal-website\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why an LMS puts more load on a server than a normal website</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">An LMS puts more load on a server than a normal website because its pages can’t be cached. Most sites are fast because the server builds a page once, saves the result, and hands that same copy to everyone who asks for it. Course pages can’t work like that, since each one shows a particular student their own progress, which is why LearnDash&#8217;s guidance is to exclude courses, lessons, topics, quizzes and groups from caching.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">So every course page is built from scratch. Your server handles those requests using PHP workers, and one worker deals with one request until that request is finished. If your plan includes four workers and five students open a lesson at the same instant, the fifth waits for a worker to come free. That is why the number of students on your site at once matters more than the number enrolled.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Video is another load, and where it’s served from decides whether it touches your server at all. Video on YouTube or Vimeo is delivered by the platform. Video uploaded to your own site is served by your server, competing with every course page request, so moving it to YouTube or Vimeo is usually the single biggest improvement available to you.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-how-to-work-out-how-many-students-will-be-on-your-site-at-once\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to work out how many students will be on your site at once</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Working out how many students will be on your site at once uses records you already have, so there is no guesswork involved.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you have launched before, open your analytics or your LMS reports for the first day of your last cohort and find how many people were active during your busiest hour. Then scale that up in proportion to how much bigger the new cohort is.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If this is your first launch, take the number of people enrolled and assume all of them arrive in the same hour. The real figure will be lower, and this gives you a ceiling to plan against.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-the-bugs-to-watch-for\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">The bugs to watch for</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The bugs to watch for are the ones that appear while your site is still up, which is what makes them difficult to catch in advance.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Video stalls or buffers partway through a lesson.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Quiz answers fail to save, so a student finishes and sees nothing recorded.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Logins loop, sending someone back to the sign-in page after they’ve already signed in.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Certificates don’t generate for students who have passed.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Progress shows against the wrong student, or does not update at all, which points at course pages being cached when they should not be.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">These reach you as support emails from students who believe the course itself is broken.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-how-to-test-it-before-you-launch\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to test it before you launch</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Testing before you launch means reproducing the arrival pattern on a copy of your site instead of on the live one. Set up a staging copy, which most managed hosts offer as a one-click option, and put a copy of your course on it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Then get as many people into it at once as you can find. Ten to twenty colleagues, friends or past students, all logging in within the same few minutes and working through the first lesson, will tell you more than any specification sheet, because it puts the load through in the shape a real launch does.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Watch three things while they do it. Does the video start promptly for the people who join last, or only for the ones who got there first? Do quiz answers save on the first attempt? Does anyone get returned to the login screen?</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For a bigger test than you can staff, load testing tools such as k6 and Loader.io simulate large numbers of visitors arriving together. They take some setting up, and they will not test video playback, so run the human version as well.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id466749_9a539e-36 alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column466749_804722-d8\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-accordion alignnone accordion\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-wrap kt-accordion-id466749_bf90e8-3d kt-accordion-has-26-panes kt-active-pane-0 kt-accordion-block kt-pane-header-alignment-left kt-accodion-icon-style-xclose kt-accodion-icon-side-right\" style=\"max-width:none\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-inner-wrap\" data-allow-multiple-open=\"false\" data-start-open=\"none\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-1 kt-pane466749_3f23cf-23\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">How many students can a WordPress LMS handle?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">That depends on your hosting and not on the LMS. LearnDash has no student limit of its own. What limits you is how many requests your server can deal with at the same time, which comes down to your PHP worker count, your database, and where your video is served from.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-2 kt-pane466749_579f11-22\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Would moving to a hosted course platform solve this?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading466749_992bd0-c2 wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading466749_992bd0-c2\">It hands the problem to someone else and charges you for it, usually as a percentage of what you sell. You also give up control of your design, your student list and your records. Staying on WordPress keeps those and leaves the capacity question with you and your host.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-3 kt-pane466749_5ea02c-c1\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Will a caching plugin make my LMS faster?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On the public parts of your site, yes. On the course pages themselves, caching causes the progress-tracking problems listed above, so those pages get excluded and their speed comes from server capacity instead.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-hosting-built-for-lms-scalability\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Hosting built for LMS scalability</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Liquid Web&#8217;s managed WordPress hosting is built for sites where visitors are logged in and pages have to be generated for each one, which is the position every LearnDash site is in. That means staging environments to run your launch test on, capacity sized for the hour everybody arrives rather than the daily average, and caching configured to leave your course pages alone.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/lms-scalability-can-your-platform-handle-it/\">LMS scalability: can your platform handle a launch?</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:7;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:66:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:53:\"How to convert a WordPress multisite to a single site\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:82:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/convert-wordpress-multisite-to-single-site-kadence/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:4:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:27:\"WordPress / Site Management\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:7:\"Kadence\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:14:\"site migration\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:19:\"WordPress multisite\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466745\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:370:\"<p>Need to unwind a WordPress multisite install? A step-by-step guide to converting back to a single site cleanly using Kadence.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/convert-wordpress-multisite-to-single-site-kadence/\">How to convert a WordPress multisite to a single site</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11239:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When a WordPress multisite network has more sites in it than you need, you can choose to separate any number of those sites out and give them WordPress installations of their own. WordPress has no built-in tool for this, because a network keeps every site&#8217;s content in its own set of database tables and every site&#8217;s images in its own folder, all inside one shared installation. Converting a WordPress multisite to a single site means copying the right pieces into a fresh installation, renaming them so the new site can find them, and turning off the settings that made it a network. The steps below assume the site you’re moving, runs on the Kadence theme, though everything except the design checks works the same way on any WordPress theme.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-before-you-start-the-conversion\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before you start the conversion</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Before you start the conversion, back up the whole network and set up a copy of it to practice on. Parts of this job touch pieces of the database that every site in the network shares, so a mistake can affect sites you were not moving, and running it through once on a copy shows you what to expect.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Four things to have ready:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>A full backup of every file and the whole database, covering the network not only your site.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A staging copy of that backup to do the work on.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Your site&#8217;s ID number, listed next to it in Network Admin > Sites. Almost everything you need to find is named after this number.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>A list of which plugins were switched on for the whole network and which were switched on for your site alone. The network ones will not come across and need installing again.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Export your Kadence settings on their own as well. With the Kadence Starter Templates plugin active, go to Appearance > Customize > Import/Export and download the .dat file, which you can load straight back in if the design does not survive the move.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-belongs-to-your-site\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What belongs to your site</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">What belongs to your site is everything named after that ID number. If your site is number 4, its database tables are called <em>wp_4_posts</em>, <em>wp_4_options </em>and so on, and its images sit in a folder called <em>wp-content/uploads/sites/4/</em>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The main site of a network works differently. It is number 1, its tables have no number in them, its permission entries don’t have a number either, and its images are already in wp-content/uploads/.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some things stay behind. Three tables called wp_blogs, wp_site and wp_sitemeta exist only to run the network, and a single site has no use for them. Accounts are the other way around, because everyone with a login anywhere on the network is stored together in wp_users and wp_usermeta, so you take only the people who had access to your site.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-move-your-site-into-a-new-wordpress-install\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Move your site into a new WordPress install</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Moving your site into a new WordPress install is more reliable than stripping the network out of the installation you already have, because you start from a copy of WordPress you know is working. Install WordPress, then install the Kadence theme and each of your plugins from their own sources before you import anything. Downloading fresh copies avoids bringing across files that were modified or left out of date, and having the theme in place first gives your imported design settings something to attach to when the database arrives.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Then move the site itself:</p>\n\n\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Export your site&#8217;s tables from the network database.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Rename each one, changing the <em>wp_4_ </em>at the start to<em> wp_.</em></li>\n\n\n\n<li>Delete the default tables in the new install, then import your renamed ones.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Copy the contents of wp-content/uploads/sites/4/ into wp-content/uploads/ on the new site.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Change the old web address to the new one everywhere it appears in the database, using WP-CLI&#8217;s search-replace command or a WordPress plugin built for this. Theme settings and widget layouts are saved in a format that an ordinary find and replace will damage.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>In the <em>wp_usermeta</em> table, rename the entries called <em>wp_4_capabilities </em>and<em> wp_4_user_level</em> to <em>wp_capabilities</em> and<em> wp_user_level</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Skipping the last step locks you out of your own site. WordPress records what each person is allowed to do separately for every site in a network and puts the site number in the name, so if those names keep the number, your new site has no administrator.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-turn-off-the-network-settings\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Turn off the network settings</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Turning off the network settings is what stops WordPress behaving like a network. Until you do it, WordPress keeps loading its multisite code and sending web addresses through the network&#8217;s routing rules, and those instructions sit in two files.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In <em>wp-config.php, </em>delete or comment out </p>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-preformatted\">MULTISITE, SUBDOMAIN_INSTALL, DOMAIN_CURRENT_SITE, PATH_CURRENT_SITE, SITE_ID_CURRENT_SITE, BLOG_ID_CURRENT_SITE and WP_ALLOW_MULTISITE. </pre>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><br>That last one is what switched the network on originally, so it goes with the others.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In <em>.htaccess</em>, <strong>delete</strong> everything between the lines marked<em> # BEGIN WordPress and # END WordPress.</em> Then open <em>Settings > Permalinks </em>on the new site and click save, and WordPress writes the correct rules back in for you.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-check-the-design-and-redirect-the-old-addresses\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Check the design and redirect the old addresses</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Checking the design and redirecting the old addresses is the last step. Open the Kadence Customizer and look at your color palette, your fonts, and the header and footer. If the site loads with no styling at all, import the .dat file you saved at the start.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Then set your site address under <em>Settings > General</em>, and put 301 redirects in place sending each old address to the one that replaces it. This is important, because if you point everything at the homepage instead you will lose the search value each page had built up prior to the conversion. Leave the old site working until you have clicked through the new one and checked that the images load.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id466745_12fd2c-48 alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column466745_a37cd8-e2\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-accordion alignnone accordion\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-wrap kt-accordion-id466745_371ed3-70 kt-accordion-has-26-panes kt-active-pane-0 kt-accordion-block kt-pane-header-alignment-left kt-accodion-icon-style-xclose kt-accodion-icon-side-right\" style=\"max-width:none\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-inner-wrap\" data-allow-multiple-open=\"false\" data-start-open=\"none\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-1 kt-pane466745_d3b151-50\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Will converting a multisite to a single site affect my search rankings?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Only if the old addresses stop working. A 301 redirect from each old address to its replacement tells search engines the page has moved for good, and the ranking it had built up moves with it.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-2 kt-pane466745_81fd75-1e\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Can I do this with the main site of the network?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading466745_ad3734-b5 wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading466745_ad3734-b5\">Yes, and there is less to do. The main site is number 1, so its tables, its permission entries and its images are already named the way a single site expects, and you can go straight to turning off the network settings.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-3 kt-pane466745_57dd49-b2\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Do I have to move all the users?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No. Accounts for the whole network are stored together, so export only the people who had access to the site you are moving, then rename their permission entries as part of the import.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where to start</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Start by finding your site&#8217;s ID number in Network Admin &gt; Sites, then build a staging copy of the network and run the whole conversion on that before you touch the live one.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When you’re moving a site out of a multisite network, it helps to have a safe place to test changes before going live. With Liquid Web’s Managed WordPress Hosting, you get staging environments, direct database access, and automatic backups, so you can handle even complex migrations with confidence.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/convert-wordpress-multisite-to-single-site-kadence/\">How to convert a WordPress multisite to a single site</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:8;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:66:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:49:\"What to look for in a nonprofit membership plugin\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:78:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/nonprofit-membership-software-what-to-look-for/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:4:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:22:\"Community & Membership\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:18:\"community building\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:21:\"membership management\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:18:\"nonprofit software\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466743\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:582:\"<p>When you&#8217;re choosing membership software for a nonprofit, you’re deciding where your member and donation records will live for the next several years. A nonprofit membership plugin keeps track of whose joined, what they can see and when they renew, and it works alongside whatever you use to take donations. Most options on the market...</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/nonprofit-membership-software-what-to-look-for/\">What to look for in a nonprofit membership plugin</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:8945:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When you&#8217;re choosing membership software for a nonprofit, you’re deciding where your member and donation records will live for the next several years. A nonprofit membership plugin keeps track of whose joined, what they can see and when they renew, and it works alongside whatever you use to take donations. Most options on the market have many of the same features, but what separates them is how easily you can get at your own records, and whether someone who pays a membership fee and also gives a donation shows up once in your system or twice.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-can-you-get-at-your-member-and-donation-records\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can you get at your member and donation records?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Getting at your member and donation records is something you should be able to do yourself, whenever you need to, without asking the software company to do it for you. You might need a list of everyone who joined this year to send them something, or a count of members and what they gave for a grant application, or the names of everyone whose membership lapsed before the renewal reminders go out.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">There are four things to cover with any supplier before you sign up:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Can you export your members, your donations and your payment history at any time, in a plain format like CSV that a spreadsheet or another system can open?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Can you filter a list before you export it, or does it only come out all at once?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>What do the terms of service say about who owns the records?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>What happens to the records if you stop paying for the software?</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-does-it-share-records-with-your-donation-tool\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Does it share records with your donation tool?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Sharing records with your donation tool is what keeps the same person from appearing twice in your system. Without it, somebody on your team ends up matching a membership renewal in one list against a donation in another. With it, one record shows you that a person is a member, when they renewed, and what they have given separately from their membership fee.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On WordPress, Paid Memberships Pro and MemberPress handle membership levels and who can see what while Give handles the donations, and the thing to check with any combination is whether a payment taken in one of them updates the same person&#8217;s record in the other automatically.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-will-your-membership-plugin-still-fit-as-you-grow\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Will your membership plugin still fit as you grow?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether your membership plugin will still fit as you grow is worth thinking about now, because moving membership software later takes weeks of somebody&#8217;s time.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Renewal season and annual appeals put a few hundred people through your site within a couple of hours, all logging in, paying and updating their details at the same time. That is a heavier load than the site handles in a normal month, so put it to the supplier and to whoever hosts your site, and ask both of them what happens on that morning.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most organizations also add something else within a year or two, whether that is event ticketing, an email tool, or a way to take regular monthly gifts. Ask which of those your membership plugin already connects to, and what setting up each connection involves.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-frequently-asked-questions\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n\n\n<div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id466743_d5fad5-56 alignnone wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column466743_a355af-bb\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-accordion alignnone accordion\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-wrap kt-accordion-id466743_b059cc-b1 kt-accordion-has-27-panes kt-active-pane-0 kt-accordion-block kt-pane-header-alignment-left kt-accodion-icon-style-xclose kt-accodion-icon-side-right\" style=\"max-width:none\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-inner-wrap\" data-allow-multiple-open=\"false\" data-start-open=\"none\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-1 kt-pane466743_ca229b-c7\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Do we need a CRM as well as a nonprofit membership plugin?<br></span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Not necessarily. Plenty of organizations run their membership and their giving on WordPress with no separate system, and a CRM tends to earn its place once you are tracking things a membership record cannot describe, such as a grant application moving through stages. What matters either way is that one person has one record.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-2 kt-pane466743_ae5caa-d1\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">Can a WordPress membership plugin handle a nonprofit as well as a hosted platform?<br></span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading466743_9949b8-04 wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading466743_9949b8-04\">Yes, given hosting that suits it. The plugin handles membership levels, access and renewals, while your hosting decides whether the site stays up when a few hundred people renew on the same morning. With WordPress you choose those two separately, which is also what lets you keep your records in a format you can use.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-3 kt-pane466743_2e3ae3-ba\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">What is the most common mistake when choosing nonprofit membership management software?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Choosing on the monthly price alone, because the subscription is usually smaller than the staff time spent moving everything across two years later when the software stops fitting. Asking early how you would get your records out is a better guide to the real cost.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-pane kt-accordion-pane kt-accordion-pane-27 kt-pane466743_63973d-0f\"><h3 class=\"kt-accordion-header-wrap\"><button class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-header kt-acccordion-button-label-show\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title-wrap\"><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-title\">What is the most common mistake when choosing nonprofit membership management software?</span></span><span class=\"kt-blocks-accordion-icon-trigger\"></span></button></h3><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel kt-accordion-panel-hidden\"><div class=\"kt-accordion-panel-inner\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Choosing on the monthly price alone, because the subscription is usually smaller than the staff time spent moving everything across two years later when the software stops fitting. Asking early how you would get your records out is a better guide to the real cost.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n</div></div>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-where-to-start\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where to start</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Start by writing down the questions you need to be able to answer about your members, things like how many joined this year, how many lapsed, and how many of them also donate. Take that list to any nonprofit membership plugin you are considering and ask them to show you where each answer comes from and how long it takes to get.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/nonprofit-membership-software-what-to-look-for/\">What to look for in a nonprofit membership plugin</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:9;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:66:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:46:\"Top LearnDash integrations and how to use them\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:54:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/learndash-integrations/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:4:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:20:\"LMS / Online Courses\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9:\"learndash\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:16:\"LMS integrations\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9:\"WordPress\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466741\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:345:\"<p>Explore the top LearnDash integrations for payments, memberships, and automation — plus how to set each one up to scale your courses.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/learndash-integrations/\">Top LearnDash integrations and how to use them</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:9104:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most LearnDash integrations do one of four jobs: taking payment for a course, gating access behind a membership, moving information between your site and the tools you already use, or changing how your courses look. WooCommerce, MemberPress, Paid Memberships Pro, Uncanny Automator, and WP Fusion cover most of what a course business needs.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Which ones you install depends on how you sell. Someone charging once per course needs a cart and nothing else, while someone running a library on a monthly fee needs a membership plugin. Below covers what each top integration does.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-selling-courses-with-the-learndash-woocommerce-integration\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Selling courses with the LearnDash WooCommerce integration</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The LearnDash WooCommerce integration connects a WooCommerce product to one or more of your courses, and when someone buys that product they are enrolled automatically. Because the purchase runs through WooCommerce, every payment gateway WooCommerce supports works, and you can attach several courses to a single product to sell a bundle.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You need LearnDash and WooCommerce installed and activated. If you want to charge monthly or yearly rather than once, you also need the WooCommerce Subscriptions extension, which is a separate paid product from WooCommerce. This is something which catches people out after they’ve already built their course, so it&#8217;s important to decide on recurring billing before you set the product up.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To connect a product to a course:</p>\n\n\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Install the integration from LearnDash LMS > Add-ons in your WordPress dashboard. This requires an active LearnDash license.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Publish your course and set its access mode to Closed, which tells LearnDash that access comes from a purchase rather than from registration.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Go to Products > Add New, or open an existing product.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>In the Product data box, click the LearnDash Courses field, search for your course, and select it. Add more courses to the same product if you are building a bundle.</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Click Update.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">There are two settings which can be changed once the basics work. Under LearnDash LMS > WooCommerce, the Enrollment Status screen lets you decide which order and subscription statuses grant access, so a refunded order or a cancelled subscription removes the student without you doing anything. The same screen has a retroactive access tool, which enrolls people who bought before you connected the product, which can be a helpful fix if you attached the course to a product that had already been selling.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-adding-recurring-revenue-with-the-learndash-membership-integration\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Adding recurring revenue with the LearnDash membership integration</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A LearnDash membership integration gates courses by plan rather than by individual purchase, so one subscription covers everything at that level instead of one course. This is the setup to use when you sell access to a growing library, run a professional body where members expect everything included, or want to keep charging after the course is finished.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Four membership plugins connect to LearnDash as free add-ons.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>MemberPress</strong> maps membership levels to courses, enrolls people when they sign up, and removes them when they cancel.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Paid Memberships Pro</strong> does the same and supports time-limited access, which matters if a certification expires after a set period.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Restrict Content Pro</strong> removes access automatically on cancellation.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>WooCommerce Memberships</strong> gates courses behind a plan inside a store you already run on WooCommerce.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Setup follows the same shape in each. Install the add-on from LearnDash LMS &gt; Add-ons, set your courses to Closed access mode, create the membership level in the plugin, then map the level to the courses it should include. Access is granted and removed by the membership plugin from that point on, so you stop managing enrollments by hand.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you sell a small number of courses one at a time you should skip this. A membership plugin adds a layer of settings to maintain and gives you nothing that a WooCommerce product does not already do.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-automating-enrollment-certificates-and-follow-up\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Automating enrollment, certificates, and follow-up</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Automation integrations watch for something happening in LearnDash and then do something else, which removes the manual steps between a student finishing a course and the next thing that should happen to them.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Uncanny Automator</strong> is free and works without code. You build a recipe by picking a trigger, such as a student completing a course, and then picking actions, such as issuing a certificate, adding them to a mailing list, and sending an email.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Zapier</strong> is also free for LearnDash and connects to more than 8,000 other applications, which is the route to take when the tool you want to reach is outside WordPress.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>WP Fusion</strong> is a premium plugin that syncs course activity into more than 100 CRMs including HubSpot, Salesforce, and Klaviyo, and it goes deeper than Zapier because it keeps records matched in both directions rather than firing one-off events.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Slack</strong> is free and pushes 13 different LearnDash events into a channel, which is a quick way to see enrollments and completions as they happen.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Start with one recipe. A common first one is: student passes the final quiz, certificate issues, tag updates in the CRM, and a follow-up email goes out asking for a review.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-other-learndash-integrations-worth-knowing\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Other LearnDash integrations worth knowing</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The rest of the library covers the parts of a course business that sit around the teaching itself.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>For community:</strong> BuddyBoss is a premium plugin that adds activity feeds, member directories, and groups, which suits cohort programs where students are meant to talk to each other. BuddyPress and bbPress are free alternatives, and bbPress grants forum access automatically when someone enrolls.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>For design.</strong> Elementor is a premium integration that gives you drag and drop course and lesson layouts. PowerPack for LearnDash is free and adds 50 modules for building course pages.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>For events and live sessions:</strong> The Events Calendar connects to LearnDash so that tying an event to a course enrolls attendees automatically, which is how live workshops and webinars get handled without a separate registration list.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>For email:</strong> &nbsp;Mailchimp and Bento are free and push student data into your email tool. Memberium connects LearnDash to Keap and grants course access based on tags.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>For video and language:</strong> Vimeo supports offline lesson downloads through the LearnDash app. Weglot is premium and translates course content.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-where-to-start\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Where to start</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Install the integration that matches how you take money first, get one course selling end to end, then add automation once you can see where the manual work is. Every plugin you add is another thing to keep updated, and the morning a cohort starts is the worst time to find out that two of them disagree.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">LearnDash is a Nexcess product, so the LMS, the integrations, and the hosting underneath them come from the same company rather than from three vendors pointing at each other when something breaks.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-managed-hosting-built-for-learndash\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Managed Hosting Built for LearnDash</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A busy course platform needs a system that can handle student checkouts, background updates, and API requests at the same time and without slowing down. Liquid Web Managed WordPress Hosting gives you the resources, caching, and database separation you need to keep LearnDash and its integrations running smoothly as you grow.<br></p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/learndash-integrations/\">Top LearnDash integrations and how to use them</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:10;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:66:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:32:\"WooCommerce hosting requirements\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:64:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/woocommerce-hosting-requirements/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:4:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11:\"WooCommerce\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:24:\"ecommerce infrastructure\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:19:\"server requirements\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:19:\"woocommerce hosting\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:35:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/?p=466738\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:345:\"<p>PHP version, memory limits, SSL, backups. Here\'s the real WooCommerce hosting requirements checklist before you launch or migrate a store. </p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/woocommerce-hosting-requirements/\">WooCommerce hosting requirements</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:15:\"Kamila Thompson\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:5881:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When you&#8217;re setting up a WooCommerce store, your hosting has to provide a few specific things before the platform will run properly. The WooCommerce server requirements are a published list of five key performance requirements you need to validate to ensure performance.  However, these are often not enough so we’ve added a few additional tips. Checking them before you launch is far easier than finding out something is missing once you’re live and taking orders.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-the-woocommerce-server-requirements\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">The WooCommerce server requirements</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The WooCommerce server requirements cover five things. Hosting that falls short on any of them will either cause a particular problem or stop WooCommerce installing at all.</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>WordPress 6.9 or later.</strong> WooCommerce is a WordPress plugin, so the WordPress hosting requirements have to be met before the WooCommerce ones come into it.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>PHP 8.3 or later.</strong> PHP is the language your store runs on, and WooCommerce is tested up to version 8.4.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>MySQL 8.0 or MariaDB 10.6 or later.</strong> This is the database holding your products, orders and customers.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>HTTPS across the site.</strong> The certificate has to cover every page rather than the checkout alone, because browsers and payment providers both treat a partly secured site as broken.</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>A memory limit of 256MB or higher.</strong> This is how much memory a single page is allowed to use while it loads.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">WooCommerce does still run on older versions of PHP and MySQL, but those stopped receiving security updates, so a store sitting on them is running unpatched. This leads to privacy and security vulnerability that retailers can’t afford.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-how-to-check-what-your-host-gives-you\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to check what your host gives you</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you&#8217;re unsure what your host gives you when you buy hosting from them, WooCommerce reports all of this back to you, so you can check. In your WordPress dashboard, go to WooCommerce &gt; Status, where the System Status report lists your PHP version, your database version and your memory limit, and flags anything below requirement in red.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Run it before you launch, before a big sale, and after your host changes anything on your account. If something comes back short, ask your host to fix it, because raising a memory limit or moving you to a newer PHP version are normal requests that don’t take long to fix.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-the-requirements-do-not-cover\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What the requirements do not cover</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The requirements do not cover what happens when lots of people buy from you at once. Three other things decide that, and hosts do not usually advertise any of them.</p>\n\n\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>The first is caching. Most websites are fast because the server builds a page once, saves the result and hands the same copy to everyone who asks for it, which works until the page in question is a shopping cart. Every shopper&#8217;s cart holds something different, so WooCommerce requires that the cart, checkout and My Account pages are left out of caching.<br></li>\n\n\n\n<li>The second is how many customers your server can serve at the same time. A cached page costs it almost nothing, while a checkout takes up a slot for as long as the customer needs to finish. Hosts call those slots PHP workers. A plan with two of them leaves eight people waiting when ten check out together.<br></li>\n\n\n\n<li>The third is how often your database is backed up. Product pages can go weeks without changing while your orders table changes with every sale. A nightly backup on a busy store means a restore that loses a day of orders. What a store needs is hourly or continuous backups, and on most plans that is a paid extra rather than the default.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-to-ask-woocommerce-hosting-providers\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to ask WooCommerce hosting providers</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When you&#8217;re comparing WooCommerce hosting providers, storage and bandwidth are what get advertised, and neither says much about how a store copes when it gets busy. Three questions get closer.</p>\n\n\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>How many PHP workers does this plan include?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>How often do you back up the database, and how far back can you restore a single order?</li>\n\n\n\n<li>Which pages does your caching exclude?</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Any host that has built for stores will have specific answers to all three.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-to-do-first\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to do first</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">What to do first is run the System Status report, which takes about thirty seconds and tells you whether you have a real problem or nothing to fix. Send your host anything it flags, then put the three questions above to whoever you are hosting with now.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-infrastructure-built-for-ecomm-requirements\" class=\"wp-block-heading\">Infrastructure built for ecomm requirements</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Online stores need infrastructure built for constant database changes. Liquid Web’s Managed WooCommerce Hosting is designed with PHP workers that scale automatically, backups that protect every order, and extra server capacity so your store stays reliable as your sales grow.</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/woocommerce-hosting-requirements/\">WooCommerce hosting requirements</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}}}i:11;a:6:{s:4:\"data\";s:63:\"\n		\n		\n		\n		\n		\n				\n		\n		\n		\n\n					\n										\n					\n		\n		\n			\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"child\";a:3:{s:0:\"\";a:6:{s:5:\"title\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:40:\"How to set up a VPS from start to finish\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"link\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:47:\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/vps-setup-guide/\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:7:\"pubDate\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:0:\"\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:8:\"category\";a:3:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:11:\"VPS Hosting\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:6:\"VMware\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:3:\"VPS\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:4:\"guid\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:40:\"https://wwwprod.lwinternal.com/?p=265750\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:1:{s:0:\"\";a:1:{s:11:\"isPermaLink\";s:5:\"false\";}}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}s:11:\"description\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:597:\"<p>Key takeaways A VPS gives you more control than shared hosting, along with greater responsibility for security and maintenance. Setup typically includes choosing the operating system and resources, connecting remotely, installing updates, securing access, and configuring the required services. This guide covers the Linux and Windows process in six steps. Get premium VPS hosting High-performance...</p>\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/vps-setup-guide/\">How to set up a VPS from start to finish</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com\">Liquid Web</a>.</p>\n\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:32:\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\";a:1:{s:7:\"creator\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:16:\"gilberthernandez\";s:7:\"attribs\";a:0:{}s:8:\"xml_base\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"xml_base_explicit\";b:0;s:8:\"xml_lang\";s:0:\"\";}}}s:40:\"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/\";a:1:{s:7:\"encoded\";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:\"data\";s:108572:\"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Key takeaways</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">VPS setup includes choosing a configuration, connecting securely, updating the system, setting up a firewall, and installing services.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Linux servers typically use SSH, while Windows servers use Remote Desktop.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Test a new administrator account before restricting root access, passwords, or firewall rules.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Configure backups and monitoring before moving production traffic to the VPS.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/vps/\">VPS</a> gives you more control than shared hosting, along with greater responsibility for security and maintenance.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Setup typically includes choosing the operating system and resources, connecting remotely, installing updates, securing access, and configuring the required services. This guide covers the Linux and Windows process in six steps.</p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column265750_4e4602-ee kb-section-dir-horizontal card-small card-light\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column265750_4dacdd-80\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<h3 class=\"kt-adv-heading265750_e51b4b-31 card-title wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-3-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading265750_e51b4b-31\">Get premium VPS hosting</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"kt-adv-heading265750_81a651-ef wp-block-kadence-advancedheading has-theme-palette-4-color has-text-color\" data-kb-block=\"kb-adv-heading265750_81a651-ef\">High-performance VPS hosting that delivers unrivaled power, the fastest speeds available, and 24/7 expert support</p>\n</div></div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-column kadence-column265750_7c4261-86\"><div class=\"kt-inside-inner-col\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn kb-buttons-wrap kb-btns265750_d8cd2c-83\"><a class=\"kb-button kt-button button kb-btn265750_b36c0e-f3 kt-btn-size-standard kt-btn-width-type-auto kb-btn-global-outline kt-btn-has-text-true kt-btn-has-svg-false wp-block-kadence-singlebtn kb-preset--outline\" href=\"/vps-hosting/\"><span class=\"kt-btn-inner-text\">Explore VPS hosting</span></a></div>\n</div></div>\n</div></div>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-vps-setup-steps-at-a-glance\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>VPS setup steps at a glance</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Choose your VPS configuration</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Provision and connect to the server</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Update the system and create an administrator</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Secure remote access and configure the firewall</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Install services and connect your domain</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Configure backups, monitoring, and final testing</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-left has-theme-palette-9-color has-text-color has-background has-link-color wp-elements-2 wp-block-paragraph\" style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgb(81,55,43) 12%,rgb(115,90,61) 52%,rgb(39,84,83) 100%)\"><em>Learn more: <a href=\"/vps/\">A complete beginner’s guide to virtual private servers →</a></em></p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-what-you-need-before-you-start\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>What you need before you start</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Before changing your server settings, make sure you have access to the provider control panel, know the operating system and version, can reach the provider’s console or recovery tools, and have the domain name you plan to use if you intend to <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/host-your-own-website/\">host a website</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You will record the server’s IP address, hostname, and connection details in Step 2. Store passwords, SSH keys, and recovery information in a secure password manager.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Also confirm that your provider offers browser-based console or recovery access. This gives you another way to reach the server if an SSH, Remote Desktop, or firewall change blocks the normal connection.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-1-choose-your-vps-configuration\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>1. Choose your VPS configuration</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Compare more than the starting price. Look at the supported operating systems, management options, resources, backup services, support scope, data center locations, and whether you can move to a <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/dedicated-server/\">dedicated server</a> as your needs grow.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-decide-whether-to-manage-the-vps-yourself\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Decide whether to manage the VPS yourself</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You can set up your own VPS if you’re comfortable using SSH or Remote Desktop and handling updates, security, <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/blog/how-to-backup-vps/\">backups</a>, monitoring, and troubleshooting.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A self-managed VPS gives you direct control over the server, but the provider may limit support to hardware, networking, and platform availability.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With a managed VPS, the provider handles a defined set of administrative tasks. Confirm whether the plan includes operating system updates, security configuration, service monitoring, backups and restoration, control-panel support, and web server troubleshooting.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A control panel can simplify hosting tasks, but it doesn’t turn a self-managed server into a managed one.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-choose-linux-or-windows\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Choose Linux or Windows</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Select the operating system based on the software you plan to run, licensing requirements, and your team’s experience.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A Linux VPS may work well for WordPress and other open source applications, <a href=\"https://httpd.apache.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Apache</a> or <a href=\"https://www.nginx.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">NGINX</a>, PHP, Python, Node.js, container workloads, and teams comfortable with command-line administration.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Common server distributions include <a href=\"https://ubuntu.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ubuntu</a>, Debian, and AlmaLinux. Use instructions written for the distribution installed on your VPS because commands and service names may differ.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A Windows VPS may work better for IIS, ASP.NET or .NET Framework applications, Microsoft SQL Server, Windows-only business software, and Remote Desktop administration.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Windows Server usually includes licensing costs that don’t apply to open source Linux distributions.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-select-the-server-resources\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Select the server resources</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Match the CPU, RAM, storage, and bandwidth to the applications you plan to run.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Leave room for the operating system, updates, security scans, background services, traffic changes, and growth. A configuration that only meets an application’s minimum requirements may need an early upgrade.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-2-provision-and-connect-to-the-server\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>2. Provision and connect to the server</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">After you select the operating system and resources, provision the VPS through the provider’s control panel.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Record the server’s:</p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Public IP address</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Hostname</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Initial username</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Temporary password, when applicable</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">SSH port or Remote Desktop information</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Provider console link</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You don’t normally need to install a hypervisor or create another virtual machine inside a hosted VPS.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-how-to-set-up-a-vps-server-on-linux\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>How to set up a VPS server on Linux</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Most administrators connect to a <a href=\"https://www.liquidweb.com/vps/what-is-linux-vps/\">Linux VPS</a> through Secure Shell, or SSH.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Current macOS, Linux, and Windows systems typically include an OpenSSH client. Open a terminal or PowerShell window and enter:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1_466217153aa.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1_466217153aa.webp.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466217\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788189594\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 1354w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1300,h_122,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 1300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1200,h_113,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 1200w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1100,h_103,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 1100w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189594/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet01-1-1024x97.webp.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1354px) 100vw, 1354px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Replace your_server_ip with the public IP address supplied by your hosting provider.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The server may ask you to confirm its host key fingerprint during the first connection. Compare it with the fingerprint shown in your hosting account before accepting it.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Enter the temporary password when prompted. The terminal won’t display the characters as you type.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">After connecting, confirm the server identity:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"153\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_153,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466218\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp\" data-crop=\"6.69\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_153,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788189640\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_153,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_149,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_134,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_119,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_104,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_89,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_74,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_59,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_44,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_29,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189640/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321/vpsstartfinish_snippet02_4662182a321.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You can specify a private SSH key when connecting:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466219\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788189673\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788189673/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096/vpsstartfinish_snippet03_4662191b096.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-how-to-set-up-a-vps-server-on-windows\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>How to set up a VPS server on Windows</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Windows VPS users commonly connect through Remote Desktop Protocol, or RDP.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To connect from a Windows computer:</p>\n\n\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Open the Remote Desktop Connection application.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Enter the VPS IP address.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Select <strong>Connect</strong>.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Enter the administrator credentials supplied by the provider.</li>\n\n\n\n<li style=\"padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\">Verify the server information before accepting a certificate warning.</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">After signing in, check the Windows Server version, computer name, IP configuration, available memory and storage, date and time, and Windows Update status.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Keep Windows Firewall enabled and limit Remote Desktop access to trusted users and networks where possible.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-3-update-the-system-and-create-an-administrator\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>3. Update the system and create an administrator</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Install available operating system updates before adding public services or moving a production workload to the VPS.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-update-ubuntu-or-debian\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Update Ubuntu or Debian</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Run:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"125\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04_4662236823d.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04_4662236823d.webp.webp\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_125,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466223\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192084\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_125,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 1354w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1300,h_158,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 1300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1200,h_146,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 1200w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1100,h_134,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 1100w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_121,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_109,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_73,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_60,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_48,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_36,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_24,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192084/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp/vpsstartfinish_snippet04-1024x125.webp.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1354px) 100vw, 1354px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The first command refreshes information about available packages. The second installs the available upgrades.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Restart the server when required:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466224\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192122\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192122/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b/vpsstartfinish_snippet05_4662248143b.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Reconnect after the restart and check for any remaining updates.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-update-almalinux-or-another-rhel-based-distribution\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Update AlmaLinux or another RHEL-based distribution</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Run:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466226\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192182\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192182/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1/vpsstartfinish_snippet06-1.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Restart the server when required, then reconnect and confirm that the services you need have started.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-update-windows-server\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Update Windows Server</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Open <strong>Settings</strong>, select <strong>Windows Update</strong>, and install the available security and quality updates.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Restart Windows when prompted, then run Windows Update again to check for additional packages.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-create-a-non-root-linux-user\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Create a non-root Linux user</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Using root for every administrative task increases the impact of a mistake or compromised login.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Create a new user:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466228\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192231\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192231/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e/vpsstartfinish_snippet07_4662286c46e.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Replace adminuser with the username you want to use.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On Ubuntu or <a href=\"https://www.debian.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Debian</a>, grant administrative privileges with:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466229\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192263\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192263/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999/vpsstartfinish_snippet08_466229e7999.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">On <a href=\"https://almalinux.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">AlmaLinux</a> and other RHEL-based systems, use the wheel group:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466230\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192288\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192288/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b/vpsstartfinish_snippet09_466230ee74b.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Open a second terminal and test the account:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466232\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192314\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192314/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet10/vpsstartfinish_snippet10.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Then verify administrative access:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466233\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192349\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192349/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702/vpsstartfinish_snippet11_46623387702.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The command should return root.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Keep the original root session open until the new account can connect and run administrative commands.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-create-a-windows-administrator-account\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Create a Windows administrator account</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Create a named account for routine Windows Server administration instead of sharing one generic administrator login.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Add the account to the appropriate local administrator group, assign a strong password, and test it through a separate Remote Desktop session.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"h-4-secure-remote-access-and-configure-the-firewall\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>4. Secure remote access and configure the firewall</strong></h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Secure remote access before opening the server to additional traffic.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 id=\"h-configure-ssh-key-authentication\" class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Configure SSH key authentication</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">SSH keys provide stronger protection than password-only access when you protect the private key with a passphrase and store it securely.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Generate an Ed25519 key on your local computer:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466235\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192414\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192414/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a/vpsstartfinish_snippet12_466235c168a.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Copy the public key to the new Linux user:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466236\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192459\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192459/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc/vpsstartfinish_snippet13_466236db3dc.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When ssh-copy-id is unavailable, add the public key to the user’s ~/.ssh/authorized_keys file manually.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Open another terminal and test the key:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466237\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192492\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192492/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9/vpsstartfinish_snippet14_466237db5d9.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Don’t change the SSH server settings until that login succeeds.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Edit the SSH configuration:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-post-265750 wp-image-466238\" data-public-id=\"prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp\" data-crop=\"10.56\" data-transformation-crop=\"w_1024,h_97,c_scale\" data-format=\"webp\" data-transformations=\"f_auto,q_auto\" data-version=\"1788192519\" data-seo=\"1\" data-responsive=\"1\" srcset=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1024,h_97,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 1024w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_1000,h_94,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 1000w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_900,h_85,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 900w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_800,h_75,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 800w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_700,h_66,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 700w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_600,h_56,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 600w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_500,h_47,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 500w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_400,h_37,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 400w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_300,h_28,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 300w, https://res.cloudinary.com/lwcom/images/w_200,h_18,c_scale/f_auto,q_auto/v1788192519/prod/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0/vpsstartfinish_snippet15_466238af1a0.webp?_i=AA 200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 40px), (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Depending on the distribution, the server may also use files inside /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Set:</p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"125\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.co